  "worked_solution": "First, notice that $T$ is a trapezoid with one pair of parallel opposite sides. Since the angles adjacent to the bases are congruent, it is an isosceles trapezoid. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given polygons.\n\n- $S$ is *not* similar to $T.$ The polygon $S$ is an isosceles trapezoid, and the corresponding angles are congruent, but the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are not the same: \n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{16}\\ne \\frac{4}{4}\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* similar to $T$ because $Q$ is a right trapezoid since it has right angles.\n\n- $P$ is similar to $T.$ Indeed, $P$ is an isosceles trapezoid. Furthermore, the corresponding angles are congruent, and the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{16}=\\frac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ only\".",
